<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuala Selangor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuala Selangor is a town at the mouth of the Selangor River on the west coast of Peninsular Malaysia. In the 18th century it was the seat of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> of Selangor, defended by a fort on Bukit Melawati overlooking the estuary. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> captured it briefly in 1784, and the royal centre later shifted towards Klang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuala-selangor.jpg" alt="Kuala Selangor" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LinEnn21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lighthouse_of_Bukit_Melawati.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Kuala Selangor means the estuary of the Selangor River. The town stands where the river drains into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, about 55 kilometres north-west of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> and 42 kilometres north-west of Shah Alam, the state capital. It is the largest town and the administrative centre of the Kuala Selangor District, which also contains the adjacent towns of Tanjung Karang, Ijok, Puncak Alam, Bestari Jaya and Jeram.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the 18th century the area was ruled by the Malacca Sultanate. In the late 17th century it was invaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a>. Kuala Selangor was then made the capital of the Sultanate of Selangor during the 18th century, a seat that was relocated three times in the years that followed.</p><p>In 1784 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquered Kuala Selangor and destroyed its fortifications. A branch railway once linked the town to Padang Jawa. It was built in stages in 1913 and 1914 and closed in 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The residents are mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, followed by Indians and ethnic Chinese. Most of the Chinese residents come from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, and the Hokkien dialect predominates among them. The town is reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> by Federal Route 54 and the Kuala Lumpur to Kuala Selangor expressway, and from the royal capital Klang by Federal Route 5.</p><p>Visitors come to Bukit Melawati, to the fireflies of Kampung Kuantan, where firefly tourism began in the early 1970s, and to the Kuala Selangor Nature Park. The park opened in 1987 and covers 296 hectares, divided between 201 hectares of tropical rainforest and a swamp forest. The town appears in the films Mukhsin (2007) and Ngangkung (2010).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuala Selangor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raja Lumu, son of the Bugis chief Daeng Chelak, was recognised as Sultan Salehuddin Shah around 1766, with the sanction of the ruler of Perak, and ruled from <b>Kuala Selangor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/selangor-sultanate">Selangor Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
